{
  "subgoal": "Compare classical generalization theories (VC theory) with the challenges posed by deep learning.",
  "content_type": "mechanism_explanation",
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "The number of parameters is not a reliable measure of complexity because a small network can approximate a complex function using only a tiny fraction of its parameters. [539, 551]",
      "sentence_ids": [
        539,
        551
      ],
      "about": "subject"
    },
    {
      "text": "VC theory posits that generalization is likely if the number of training data points is much larger than the number of distinct functions in the hypothesis space. [560, 561]",
      "sentence_ids": [
        560,
        561
      ],
      "about": "subject"
    },
    {
      "text": "The VC dimension (d) is defined as the maximum number of unique dichotomies (binary classifications) a model can apply to the training data. [617, 619]",
      "sentence_ids": [
        617,
        619
      ],
      "about": "subject"
    },
    {
      "text": "Neural networks can achieve any possible dichotomy over a dataset, which results in an exponentially loose generalization bound that fails to explain deep learning's success. [635, 647]",
      "sentence_ids": [
        635,
        647
      ],
      "about": "subject"
    }
  ]
}

{
  "subgoal": "Compare representation learning methods and explain the power of self-supervised learning.",
  "content_type": "mechanism_explanation",
  "key_points": [
    {
      "text": "Autoencoders do not strictly produce better representations; they produce different representations that involve trade-offs between tasks.",
      "sentence_ids": [
        753,
        755
      ],
      "about": "subject"
    },
    {
      "text": "K-means clustering can be viewed as an L2 autoencoder where the bottleneck is an integer (one-hot code) rather than a low-dimensional vector.",
      "sentence_ids": [
        823,
        825
      ],
      "about": "subject"
    },
    {
      "text": "Self-supervised learning uses supervised methods (predict y from x) but defines y as a part of the raw data rather than a human-assigned label.",
      "sentence_ids": [
        861
      ],
      "about": "subject"
    },
    {
      "text": "Deep networks trained via self-supervision learn fundamental semantic concepts (like objects and words) regardless of the specific prediction task.",
      "sentence_ids": [
        905,
        906
      ],
      "about": "subject"
    }
  ]
